when markets get choppy investors can  pull out to save their money or they can  cast their lines for discounted stock  aiming for a bigger profit later on this  second option is called buying the dip  and it's been a popular strategy in the  markets for years now it was even touted  during the crash of 2008. this is not a  time to be selling your stocks it's  times like these where people really  make money because stocks become a  better value but so far in 2022 buying  the dip hasn't paid off as well as in  the past markets are volatile and in  this environment buying the dip can be  risky what we're seeing is that after  people step in to buy the stock market  dip the s p 500 has kept falling the day  after so that means that you're sitting  on a loss pretty quickly  but even these circumstances haven't  stopped investors who want to bring in a  big catch here's why it's such an  attractive strategy but nearly  impossible to time  let's zoom out buying the dip is when  investors purchase assets after they  have dropped in price on the assumption  that their value will bounce back what  we've seen over the past few decades  is that sharp downturns in the stock  market have often been followed by these  huge rebounds so people that have sat on  the sidelines during say the financial  crisis or the march 2020 covet crash  have watched stocks kind of go up after  those instances  investors willing to dive in tend to  have one of three main motivations first  some investors want to buy into growing  companies they care less about the price  of the stock and more about the  opportunity to reel in a company they  think will experience significant growth  in the future other investors look for  stocks with upward momentum meaning  rising prices they're most interested in  timing the bottom of the dip so they can  maximize profits and then there's  investors who want cheap stocks they  don't care as much about how fast and  how far their stocks will grow but  they're excited that stocks that were  previously expensive are now cheap  enough for them to grab  mainly they think the stocks they're  buying will keep going up whatever their  motivation if a lot of investors attempt  this strategy the results can have  another effect one that has played out  in the past few years as more individual  investors have entered the market  individual investors have become such a  prominent force in markets in recent  years they've stampeded into the markets  they've bought a record amount of stocks  so some analysts and strategists do say  that they can kind of provide a backstop  to the market and help support the  market  during volatility sometimes when enough  investors try to catch cheap stocks they  can actually reverse the downward  trajectory take for example the 2008  crash after the housing bubble burst it  sent markets into a tailspin but by 2009  many investors began re-entering the  market pushing up the prices of cheap  stocks investors halted a three-day  losing streak sending stocks broadly  higher on a weaker dollar and better  than expected home sales numbers it was  known as the 2009 dash for trash we saw  that that prolonged downturn that steep  sell-off was was followed by an  incredible recovery an 11-year bull  market for stocks the msci all-country  world index ended up returning its best  annual performance on record in 2009  just after its worst performance in  2008.  people who said the stock market is too  scary for me right now they missed out  on those mammoth mammoth gains and and  may have regretted it strategists say  that since then investors have been  conditioned to buy the dip and it's made  individual investors money at least  until 2022. since the federal reserve  has made the biggest interest rate  increase since 2000 some think a  recession is ahead this is a huge shift  for financial markets and that's one of  the key reasons we've seen this  volatility it's an example of why it can  be so hard for investors to time the dip  because stocks can keep on falling vanda  research estimates the average  individual investor portfolio peaked  late in 2021 and then tumbled losing an  average of about 28 by may 9th  with all the dangers associated with  buying the dip it's no wonder that  analysts consider it risky if you choose  to buy the dip  you can't expect you know the market to  bounce immediately it could take days  weeks months or even years for it to  rebound and for you to pocket a profit  on that trade  [Music]
 

